  "response": "I’ll run the scan and inspect the three modules, then group the proposals by finding type and check which warnings reflect real contract ambiguity.\n\n\n`crimes scan --format json` reproduced **76 findings**. The three requested files contain **23**, including **8 in the requested categories**. Source inspection shows several warnings conflate nested callbacks or overlook existing types. No files changed.\n\n| Finding type | Findings and locations | Severity |\n|---|---|---|\n| `name_behavior_mismatch` | `crime_00003`: `getRollupConfig`, `src/rollup.ts:38–159`; `crime_00008`: `buildStart`, `:77–81` | Low |\n| `name_behavior_mismatch` | `crime_00014`: `build`, `src/index.ts:167–459`; `crime_00015`: `buildAll`, `:291–365` | Medium / low |\n| `option_bag_junk_drawer` | `crime_00007`: `getRollupConfig`, `src/rollup.ts:38–159` | Low |\n| `return_shape_roulette` | `crime_00011`: `sveltePlugin`, `src/esbuild/svelte.ts:9–124`; `crime_00012`: `setup`, `:17–122`; `crime_00013`: async load callback, `:31–121` | Low |\n\n1. **Misleading names: expose effects and preserve lifecycle contracts.**\n\n   In [rollup.ts](/private/var/folders/8_/6tdb3p2s63v_xxb15r46cqwr0000gn/T/crimes-eval-agent-P9s9a4/src/rollup.ts:38), `getRollupConfig` changes global logging, reads package metadata, and mutates `options.dts.entry`. It registers deletion for a later `buildStart` hook; it does **not** delete files during configuration construction.\n\n   Rename it `prepareDtsRollupConfig`, move logging configuration to the runner, and extract immutable entry normalization plus `createDeclarationCleanupPlugin`. Keep the framework-required name `buildStart`; delegate its body to `removeDeclarationOutputs`.\n\n   In [index.ts](/private/var/folders/8_/6tdb3p2s63v_xxb15r46cqwr0000gn/T/crimes-eval-agent-P9s9a4/src/index.ts:291), rename internal `buildAll` to `runJavaScriptBuildCycle`: it stops previous success processes, cleans outputs, builds formats, copies assets, and runs success hooks. Preserve public `build` for compatibility, explicitly type it `Promise<void>`, and document its build/watch effects. “Build” is already conventional for an effectful API; its warning alone does not justify an API rename.\n\n   **Agent risk today:** treating configuration preparation as repeatable and mutation-free, moving deletion to the wrong lifecycle phase, or assuming `buildAll` also runs declaration builds. During extraction, preserve the declaration exclusions that prevent concurrent JS cleanup from deleting declaration output.\n\n2. **Junk-drawer options: narrow ownership and make normalization real.**\n\n   `getRollupConfig` already accepts **`NormalizedOptions`** and returns **`Promise<RollupConfig>`**. The problem is breadth and mutation, rather than an entirely implicit contract.\n\n   Introduce focused internal records for declaration inputs/compiler settings, output settings, and cleanup policy. Keep watch and logging settings with orchestration. Construct these explicitly instead of forwarding the whole build configuration.\n\n   Replace the worker’s broad options spread plus function blacklist with a named, explicitly constructed `DtsWorkerRequest`. Account for `outExtension`: its callback currently receives the full options object and is stripped before worker transfer. Narrowing types must preserve that behavior or deliberately redesign it.\n\n   Give `normalizeOptions` an explicit `Promise<NormalizedOptions>` return and construct a complete value without `as NormalizedOptions`. For example, its required `tsconfigResolvePaths` currently receives a value only when a tsconfig exists. Replace compiler-option `any` with a JSON-input boundary and a parsed `ts.CompilerOptions` result.\n\n   **Agent risk today:** passing unnormalized settings, adding an uncloneable callback to worker messages, or mutating shared configuration. Existing examples include assigning through `dtsOptions.entry` and calling `unshift` on the caller’s `clean` array during rebuilds.\n\n3. **Multiple return shapes: type each callback boundary separately.**\n\n   In [svelte.ts](/private/var/folders/8_/6tdb3p2s63v_xxb15r46cqwr0000gn/T/crimes-eval-agent-P9s9a4/src/esbuild/svelte.ts:9), `sveltePlugin` already returns `Plugin`; `setup` registers callbacks and returns nothing. The scanner combines resolver results, diagnostics, preprocessing results, and load results.\n\n   Extract named helpers with explicit contracts:\n\n   - `resolveSvelteCss`: esbuild `OnResolveResult`.\n   - `loadSvelteComponent`: `Promise<OnLoadResult>`.\n   - `toEsbuildDiagnostic`: `PartialMessage`, accepting validated diagnostics or narrowed `unknown` errors instead of `any`.\n   - `preprocessSvelteScript`: the installed Svelte preprocessor contract.\n   - `setup`: explicit `void`.\n\n   Preserve esbuild’s valid success/error result shapes. Introduce a discriminated union only for an internal compilation result if useful; do not combine unrelated framework contracts into one union.\n\n   **Agent risk today:** returning `{ code, map }` where esbuild expects `{ contents }`, confusing a diagnostic with a load result, or accessing `contents` on an error result.\n\nThe remaining findings support these extractions: **10 `large_function` findings** (`00048`, `00051–53`, `00059`, `00062–63`, `00065–66`, `00071`), **`large_file`** (`00072`), and **connectivity** (`00070`, `00075`). Connectivity alone warrants no restructuring. Rollup also has **clock reads** (`00016`) and **swallowed-error** (`00027`): the latter overlooks `handleError(error)`, which reports the error, but failure can still fall through to a subsequent success message.\n\nImplement narrow types and callback contracts first, then extract lifecycle helpers. Validate cleanup across repeated builds, unchanged input options, worker failure signaling, and Svelte CSS/TypeScript/error behavior; run the build and relevant tests, then rescan and review residual heuristic warnings.",
